\chapter{The CRTP Foundation: \code{dsl::DSL<Derived, Features...>}}
\label{ch:crtp-foundation}

\section{Twenty Lines That Carry the Whole Library}

Every DSL built with \DSLtk{} inherits from the same twenty-line class.
Listing~\ref{lst:ch04-dsl} is that class, transcribed from the shipped header
at lines 5965--5979 without alteration.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={\code{dsl::DSL}, the CRTP base of every \DSLtk{} DSL, from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 5965--5979. The listing is shown as it appears inside \code{namespace dsl}.},label={lst:ch04-dsl}]
template <typename Derived, typename... Features>
struct DSL : Features::template Mixin<Derived>...
{
protected:
  constexpr Derived &
  self () noexcept
  {
    return static_cast<Derived &> (*this);
  }
  constexpr const Derived &
  self () const noexcept
  {
    return static_cast<const Derived &> (*this);
  }
};
\end{lstlisting}

Fifteen of those lines are the protected \code{self()} accessor and its
constant overload. The other five do all the work: a pack of feature tags is
expanded into a list of base classes, one base class per feature, each
parameterised by the deriving type. Everything else in \DSLtk{} -- the
pipeline, the AST helpers, the rewrite engine, the expression operators --
is delivered through that one mechanism.

This chapter explains the mechanism in full. It looks at how the mixin
expansion is performed, why the mixins are inherited separately rather than
gathered into a single aggregate, what the \code{template} disambiguator in
the base list is for, how a feature reaches back into the deriving type both
statically and dynamically, what the arrangement costs at run time (nothing),
and how you write a feature tag of your own.

\section{How the Mixin Expansion Works}

The base-clause
\code{Features::template Mixin<Derived>\ldots} is a pack expansion over
base classes. For a deriving type \code{D} and a feature list
\code{F1, F2, F3}, the compiler instantiates \code{F1::Mixin<D>},
\code{F2::Mixin<D>} and \code{F3::Mixin<D>} and makes each of them a direct
base class of \code{dsl::DSL<D, F1, F2, F3>}. The expansion algorithm below states the process.

\begin{pseudocode}[Expanding a feature list into base classes]
\textbf{function} expand (D, F1, F2, \ldots, Fn)\\
\quad \textbf{for} each feature \code{Fi} in the list\\
\qquad \textbf{require} \code{Fi} declares a nested template \code{Mixin}\\
\qquad instantiate \code{Mi = Fi::Mixin<D>}\\
\qquad add \code{Mi} to the base list of \code{dsl::DSL<D, F1, \ldots, Fn>}\\
\quad \textbf{return} the class with that base list
\end{pseudocode}

Two properties of this expansion matter. First, the parameterisation is by
the \emph{deriving} type, not by the feature type. A feature's mixin is
written once, as a template, and is instantiated afresh for every DSL that
names it. The \code{Pipeline} mixin is the same code for every user; what
differs is the \code{Derived} it was instantiated with, and therefore the
members it can see.

Second, the expansion is textual in the language's sense: the base list of
\code{dsl::DSL<D, F1, F2>} literally contains \code{F1::Mixin<D>} and
\code{F2::Mixin<D>}. That is what makes the feature-detection concept of
Section~\ref{sec:ch04-concepts} possible, and it is the reason the mixins
are not gathered into a single aggregate mixin.

\section{Why Separate Base Classes}\label{sec:ch04-separate}

It would have been possible to write the base class as a single aggregate
mixin holding a tuple of feature mixins:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The design that was not used: one aggregate mixin holding every feature.},label={lst:ch04-aggregate}]
template <typename Derived, typename... Features>
struct DSL : AggregateMixin<Derived, Features...> {};
\end{lstlisting}

The shipped design rejects that, for four reasons that are worth
understanding because they generalise.

\paragraph{Feature detection requires the feature to be a base.}
The library needs to answer the question \emph{``does this type carry the
\code{Visitors} feature?''}. It answers it with
\code{std::derived_from<T, F::template Mixin<T>>}, and that expression is
only well formed if \code{F::Mixin<T>} is a direct base of \code{T}. With an
aggregate mixin there is no such base: \code{Visitors::Mixin<T>} would be a
member of a tuple, and the question would have to be answered by trait
machinery instead of by the type system.

\paragraph{Each feature constrains itself independently.}
\code{Rewrite}'s mixin declares \code{rewrite} with the constraint
\code{requires requires { Derived::rules; }}, and \code{CustomLiterals}'
mixin declares \code{parse_literal} with \code{requires requires {
Derived::literals; }}. If all features lived in one aggregate, a constraint
failure in one feature would remove the member from the whole class rather
than from one feature's contribution.

\paragraph{Hidden friends stay attached to their feature.}
\code{ExprTemplates}' operators are hidden friends declared inside
\code{ExprTemplates::Mixin<Derived>}. Because the mixin is a distinct base
class, the friends it declares are found by argument-dependent lookup
exactly when one of the arguments is the deriving type -- and only that
feature's friends are found that way. Chapter~\ref{ch:feature-tags} works
through the mechanism in detail.

\paragraph{Ambiguity is a feature, not a bug.}
With separate bases, two features that inject a member of the same name
produce an ambiguity error at the point of use, naming both candidates. With
one aggregate, the second definition would silently shadow the first, and
the resulting behaviour would depend on the order of the pack. A sharp
error is worth more than a silent surprise.

\section{The \code{template} Disambiguator}

The word \code{template} in \code{Features::template Mixin<Derived>} is
not decoration. \code{Features} is a pack of unknown types at the point
where the base clause is parsed, so \code{Mixin} is a \emph{dependent
name}: the compiler cannot yet know whether it names a template, and if so
whether it takes one argument or two, or whether it takes a non-type
parameter. The standard therefore requires the \code{template} keyword to
disambiguate the name and tell the parser that a template argument list
follows.

Without it, the base clause would not parse on a strictly conforming
compiler. In practice GCC 16 and Clang 22 both accept the shorter spelling
\code{Features::Mixin<Derived>\ldots} in a base-specifier-list without
diagnostic, even under \code{-pedantic-errors}; the standard's requirement
nonetheless stands, and the shipped header uses the conforming form. The
same rule is why user code that names a member of a dependent base must
write \code{Base::template member}.

\section{Reaching \code{Derived}: Statically and Dynamically}

A feature's mixin is instantiated with the deriving type in hand, but it is
not \emph{the} deriving type: it is a base class of it. Two mechanisms
bridge that gap, and the library uses both.

\subsection{Statically, Through the Class Template Parameter}

The mixin's template parameter \code{Derived} is a complete type name at the
point of use inside the mixin, so the mixin can name static members of the
deriving class directly. This is how \code{Rewrite} finds the rewrite set
and how \code{CustomLiterals} finds the literal table.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The two features that reach \code{Derived} statically, transcribed from the header. The listings are shown as they appear inside \code{namespace dsl}.},label={lst:ch04-static}]
struct Rewrite
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    ASTNode
    rewrite (const ASTNode &tree) const
      requires requires { Derived::rules; }
    {
      return Derived::rules.apply (tree);
    }
  };
};

struct CustomLiterals
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    static long double
    parse_literal (std::string_view input)
      requires requires { Derived::literals; }
    {
      return Derived::literals.parse (input);
    }
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

Note the \code{requires requires} idiom. The outer \code{requires} is the
constraint on the member; the inner one is a \emph{requirement}, which asks
whether the expression \code{Derived::rules} is well formed. Because
\code{Derived} is a dependent type, that expression cannot be evaluated
until the mixin is instantiated, and the requirement is what makes the
constraint well formed. If the deriving class does not declare \code{rules},
the member simply does not exist -- and calling it produces the diagnostic
\emph{``constraints not satisfied''} rather than a confusing error about a
missing member.

\subsection{Dynamically, Through a Cast}

When a feature needs the deriving object itself -- to read or modify an
instance member -- it must cast. \code{Memoization}'s \code{clear_cache}
does exactly that:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={\code{Memoization}'s instance member, transcribed from the header at lines 7290--7301, and shown as it appears inside \code{namespace dsl}.},label={lst:ch04-dynamic}]
struct Memoization
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    /// Clears all cached entries stored in `Derived::memo_cache`.
    void
    clear_cache ()
    {
      static_cast<Derived &> (*this).memo_cache.clear ();
    }
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

The cast is a \code{static_cast}, not a \code{dynamic_cast}, because the
relationship between a mixin and the deriving type is known at compile time.
There is no run-time check and no runtime cost.

\subsection{What \code{self()} Is For}

The protected \code{self()} accessor exists for the \emph{deriving class},
not for the mixins. \code{self()} is declared \code{protected} in
\code{dsl::DSL}, so it is accessible in the member functions of
\code{dsl::DSL} and of any class derived from it -- that is, in your own DSL
type -- but \emph{not} in the mixins. A mixin is a base of \code{dsl::DSL},
not a derived class of it, and a base class cannot reach the protected
members of its own derived class. The compiler says so plainly:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={A mixin that tries to call \code{self()}. The diagnostic names the real problem: \code{self} is a member of \code{dsl::DSL}, not of the mixin.},label={lst:ch04-selferr}]
error: 'const struct mydsl::Length::Mixin<Derived>' has no member named 'self'
\end{lstlisting}

That is why the library's own mixins write
\code{static_cast<Derived&>(*this)} instead. \code{self()} is a
convenience for your code:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Using the protected \code{self()} accessor from the deriving class.},label={lst:ch04-selfuse}]
struct Document : dsl::DSL<Document, dsl::Pipeline>
{
  std::string_view body { "the quick brown fox" };

  std::string_view
  text () const noexcept
  {
    return self ().body;
  }
};
\end{lstlisting}

\section{The CRTP Pattern and Its Objections}

The curiously recurring template pattern is old enough to have acquired a
reputation, and the reputation is not entirely flattering. The standard
objection is that CRTP is inheritance without polymorphism: it gives you
the self-referential part of inheritance -- the ability to name your own
type from within a base class -- without the substitutability that
polymorphism promises. A base class that knows its derived type can call
the derived type's members, but the derived type is not substitutable for
the base, and no run-time decision is ever made about which derived type
you have.

That objection is correct, and \DSLtk{} accepts it. The design goal is not
polymorphism; it is the opposite. A DSL is a single, concrete type, known
statically, whose behaviour is assembled from independent parts before the
program runs. There is no base to hold polymorphically, and no reason to.

The second common objection is that CRTP is a hack, and that a language
with proper mixins would do the job better. This is the objection the D
port answers: D has template mixins, and the D implementation of \DSLtk{}
uses them, injecting members directly into the deriving struct rather than
inheriting them from a CRTP base. The D approach is arguably cleaner. The
C++ approach has one advantage that is easy to overlook: the mixins end up
as \emph{base classes}, which is what makes
\code{HasFeature<T, F>} a one-line concept rather than a trait
specialisation.

The third objection concerns the \code{static_cast} in \code{self()}. It
is, strictly speaking, a downcast, and if the object is not really of the
derived type the cast is undefined behaviour. In \DSLtk{} the cast is
always sound, because the mixin and the deriving type are related by
inheritance in exactly one way: \code{dsl::DSL<Derived, Features...>} has
\code{Features::Mixin<Derived>} as a base, and \code{Derived} derives from
\code{dsl::DSL<Derived, Features...>}. The \code{static_cast} from the
mixin to \code{Derived} is therefore a downcast through a known,
non-virtual inheritance chain, and it is well defined.

\section{Name Hiding, Not Overriding}

Because the mixin machinery is ordinary C++ inheritance, the usual rules
of name lookup apply, and they are worth spelling out because they are a
frequent source of confusion.

If your deriving class declares a member with the same name as one
contributed by a mixin, the derived declaration \emph{hides} the inherited
one. It does not override it, and there is no dispatch between them. A
call \code{obj.rewrite(tree)} on an object of your class resolves to your
declaration, and the mixin's \code{rewrite} is reachable only by an
explicit qualification such as
\code{obj.dsl::Rewrite::Mixin<MyDSL>::rewrite(tree)}.

There is one case in which both survive: if the inherited member is a
function template and the derived member is not, or vice versa, the two
overload rather than hide. A mixin that contributes
\code{make_leaf<Tag>(T&&)} and a derived class that contributes a
non-template \code{make_leaf(T&&)} will therefore produce an ambiguity
at the call site rather than a silent shadowing.

The practical advice is simple. Choose member names for your DSL that do
not collide with the names the features contribute, and if you must
override one, use a \code{using} declaration to bring the base version
into scope deliberately.

\section{No Virtual Dispatch, No Vtable}

Nothing in the mixin hierarchy is virtual. The mixins declare ordinary
member functions; \code{dsl::DSL} declares ordinary member functions; the
deriving class inherits from them. There is no \code{virtual} keyword
anywhere in the feature machinery, and therefore no vtable pointer, no
virtual base offsets, and no indirect call.

The consequence is that a feature's members are resolved at compile time
against a type the compiler knows exactly. \code{Rewrite}'s
\code{rewrite(const ASTNode&)} is not a virtual call that might be
overridden; if your class declares \code{rewrite}, it hides the mixin's, and
the compiler tells you so at the point of use. Overriding is a
compile-time concept, expressed with name hiding or with the
\code{using} declaration, exactly as it is for any ordinary non-virtual
base.

The cost is that you cannot treat a DSL as a polymorphic base and dispatch
on it at run time. If you need that -- say, to hold heterogeneous DSLs in a
container -- you must add your own virtual interface or your own type-erasing
wrapper. \DSLtk{} does not provide one, and the library's design does not
suggest that it should: the whole point of the toolkit is that the
composition happens before the program runs.

\section{The Cost Model}

The runtime cost of the whole arrangement is zero, and it is worth being
precise about why.

Every mixin is an empty class in the sense that matters: it has no data
members. \code{Pipeline::Mixin} has only a static member function.
\code{Operators::Mixin} has only a static member function. \code{AST::Mixin}
has only static member functions. \code{Rewrite::Mixin},
\code{Memoization::Mixin} and \code{CustomLiterals::Mixin} have instance
member functions but no data members of their own -- they read the deriving
class's members. The nineteen marker mixins are empty in the strictest
sense.

Because a base class subobject with no data members may be overlaid on any
other base class subobject, the \emph{empty base optimisation} guarantees
that a DSL type is the same size as it would be without the features. The
deriving class's own data members determine the layout, and the mixin bases
occupy no space at all.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch04-custom} demonstrates this. \code{PlainText} and
\code{Document} hold the same single \code{std::string_view}; the second is
\code{PlainText} lifted into a DSL that carries the \code{Length} feature.
Both are sixteen bytes.

\section{The Utility Concepts}\label{sec:ch04-concepts}

Lines 11379--11403 of the header are a short section titled \emph{``Utility
concepts''}. It contains five concepts, and they are the library's only
formal statement about what a feature \emph{is}. Table~\ref{tab:ch04-concepts}
gives them verbatim.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{The five utility concepts of \code{DSLtk.hpp}, lines 11383--11403.}
\label{tab:ch04-concepts}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.19\textwidth}L{0.52\textwidth}C{0.16\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Concept} & \textbf{Definition} & \textbf{Line} \\
\midrule
\code{HasFeature<T,F>} & \code{std::derived_from<T, typename F::template Mixin<T>>} & 11385 \\
\code{Pipeable<T>} & \code{HasFeature<T, Pipeline>} & 11389 \\
\code{ExprLike<T>} & \code{HasFeature<T, ExprTemplates>} and \code{requires (const T& t) { t.expr_value (); }} & 11393 \\
\code{HasLiterals<T>} & \code{HasFeature<T, CustomLiterals>} and \code{requires { T::literals; }} & 11398 \\
\code{Rewritable<T>} & \code{HasFeature<T, Rewrite>} and \code{requires { T::rules; }} & 11403 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The differences between them are instructive. \code{Pipeable} is a pure
feature test: it asks only whether \code{Pipeline}'s mixin is a base.
\code{ExprLike} adds an \emph{instance} requirement, because the expression
template machinery needs \code{expr_value()} on an object.
\code{HasLiterals} and \code{Rewritable} add a \emph{static} requirement,
because those two features read \code{T::literals} and \code{T::rules}. A
type that names the feature but does not supply the corresponding member
satisfies \code{HasFeature} and fails the derived concept.

There is a mild inconsistency worth noting: \code{HasLiterals} and
\code{Rewritable} use the unqualified requirement \code{requires { T::literals;
}}, which would be ill-formed for a dependent \code{T} in the absence of
\code{typename} if the member were a type rather than a value. Because the
members are values, the expressions are well formed and the concepts
compile. A reader extending this section should be careful to keep the
members values.

The concepts are also a good demonstration of the library's own idiom: they
are constrained on the \emph{feature list}, not on the presence of
particular functions. That is the payoff of the separate-base-class design
discussed in Section~\ref{sec:ch04-separate}.

\section{Writing a Custom Feature Tag}

A custom feature is two things: a tag struct, and a nested \code{Mixin}
template. Nothing else is required. The tag needs no members, no
specialisation, and no registration with the library; the mixin is
parameterised by the deriving type and can contain anything.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch04-custom} is a complete, compilable program that
defines a feature tag called \code{Length} in the user's own namespace,
uses it, and checks the library's own concepts against the result.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A custom feature tag with a nested \code{Mixin}, and the resulting DSL type. The mixin contributes an instance member and reaches the deriving type with a \code{static_cast}.},label={lst:ch04-custom}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>
#include <type_traits>

namespace mydsl {

/// Feature tag: measures the length of the derived DSL's text.
struct Length
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    std::size_t
    size () const noexcept
    {
      return static_cast<const Derived &> (*this).text ().size ();
    }
  };
};

} // namespace mydsl

/// A plain value type with no feature machinery at all.
struct PlainText
{
  std::string_view body { "the quick brown fox" };
  std::string_view
  text () const noexcept
  {
    return body;
  }
};

/// The same value type, lifted into a DSL that carries the Length feature.
struct Document : dsl::DSL<Document, mydsl::Length>
{
  std::string_view body { "the quick brown fox" };
  std::string_view
  text () const noexcept
  {
    return body;
  }
};

static_assert (dsl::HasFeature<Document, mydsl::Length>);
static_assert (!dsl::HasFeature<PlainText, mydsl::Length>);
static_assert (!std::is_polymorphic_v<Document>);

int main ()
{
  Document doc;
  std::cout << "size() = " << doc.size () << "\n";
  std::cout << "sizeof(PlainText) = " << sizeof (PlainText) << "\n";
  std::cout << "sizeof(Document)  = " << sizeof (Document) << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch04-custom}. The feature adds a member and costs nothing in memory.},label={lst:ch04-custom-out}]
size() = 19
sizeof(PlainText) = 16
sizeof(Document)  = 16
\end{lstlisting}

Three points are worth drawing out. The mixin reaches the deriving type
with \code{static_cast<const Derived&>(*this)}, exactly as the library's
own mixins do. The \code{static_assert}s show that
\code{HasFeature} distinguishes a type that carries the feature from one
that merely has the same shape. And the \code{sizeof} lines show that the
feature is free.

