\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

\paragraph{A feature tag must define a nested \code{Mixin}.}
The base clause names \code{Features::template Mixin<Derived>}, so a tag
without that nested template is ill-formed at the point of instantiation:
\emph{``no class template named 'Mixin' in 'struct NoMixin'''}. The
requirement is checked when the \code{dsl::DSL} specialisation is formed, not
when the tag is declared, so the diagnostic appears at the point of use.

\paragraph{A tag's \code{Mixin} may only be parameterised by \code{Derived}.}
The expansion is \code{F::Mixin<Derived>} with exactly one argument. A tag
whose mixin takes a different number of template parameters will not
compile, and there is no mechanism in the library for passing extra
parameters to a feature.

\paragraph{Features must be distinct.}
Repeating a tag produces the error \emph{``duplicate base type''}. The
message is not a diagnosis, but the cause is unambiguous: the expansion
would produce the same base class twice.

\paragraph{\code{self()} is protected, and only the deriving class can use
it.}
A mixin that tries to call \code{this->self()} fails to compile, because a
base class cannot reach the protected members of a class derived from it.
Use \code{static_cast<Derived&>(*this)} inside a mixin and \code{self()}
inside the deriving class.

\paragraph{The deriving type must be complete at the point of use, not at the
point of declaration.}
\code{dsl::DSL<Document, F>} may name an incomplete \code{Document}; the
base class does not require it to be complete. But the moment a member of
the mixin is instantiated -- that is, the moment you call it -- the
deriving type must be complete. In practice this means the mixin's member
bodies are always instantiated after the deriving class is closed, which is
why the pattern works at all.

\paragraph{There is no run-time feature selection.}
The feature list is part of the type. Two DSL types that differ in their
feature lists are unrelated types, and no amount of run-time configuration
will make one behave like the other. If you need a configurable DSL, make
the feature list a template parameter of your own type.

\paragraph{Name hiding, not overriding.}
Because nothing is virtual, a member declared in the deriving class hides
the mixin's member rather than overriding it. A member function template in
the mixin and a non-template member of the same name in the deriving class
can coexist, in which case the deriving class's declaration wins outright
and the mixin's is still reachable by an explicit qualification. This is
ordinary C++ name lookup, but it surprises readers who expect polymorphism.

\paragraph{Concepts are not exhaustive.}
The five utility concepts cover five features. There is no
\code{HasVisitors}, no \code{HasRewrite}, no \code{HasParsers}. The
general-purpose \code{HasFeature<T, F>} works for every tag, including
your own, but it tests only that the mixin is a base -- not that the
deriving class supplies the members the feature needs.

\section{What Comes Next}

The foundation is now open. You know how a feature list becomes a set of
base classes, what it costs, and how to add your own. Chapter
\ref{ch:fixed-string} turns to the smallest and most frequently used piece
of the library: \code{dsl::FixedString}, the type that lets a string literal
be a template argument.
